nches of hand room</text><text x="725" y="478" font-size="18" fill="#6E645C">to reach in and lift clothes out</text><line x1="680" y1="515" x2="1100" y2="515" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="2"/><text x="680" y="565" font-size="30" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">=</text><text x="725" y="565" font-size="21" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Lowest safe height for</text><text x="725" y="593" font-size="21" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">the bottom of the shelf</text><rect x="680" y="630" width="420" height="90" rx="12" fill="#DCE5D6"/><text x="890" y="665" font-size="17" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">Tip: check the spec sheet for the</text><text x="890" y="692" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A" text-anchor="middle">height with the lid open (A + B)</text><text x="600" y="920" font-size="20" fill="#6E645C" text-anchor="middle">Measure with the lid fully open before you drill a single hole.</text></svg><figcaption style="text-align:center;font-size:0.9em;color:#6E645C;">A top-loader needs open air above it, not just floor space.</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Once you know that height, everything above it is usable storage, and everything beside and around the machines becomes your main organizing space.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Plan a Small Laundry Room Around a Top-Loader</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Measure with the lid open.</strong> Record measurements A and B from the guide above, plus the width of each gap beside and between your machines.</li>



<li><strong>Mark the no-storage zone.</strong> Use painter&#8217;s tape on the wall to mark the lowest safe shelf height, so you can see it before you drill.</li>



<li><strong>Decide what lives where.</strong> Daily supplies go beside the machines, backups go on high shelves, and the walls handle drying and hanging.</li>



<li><strong>Keep the lid clear.</strong> Plan a home for everything that currently sits on top of the washer.</li>
</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">15 Small Laundry Room Ideas With a Top Loading Washer</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Above the Machines</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-02-lid-clearance.jpg" alt="Top-loading washer with the lid fully open under a floating shelf mounted high enough to clear the lid" class="wp-image-694" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-02-lid-clearance.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-02-lid-clearance-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-02-lid-clearance-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-02-lid-clearance-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>1. Hang floating shelves above the lid line.</strong> Mount open shelves just above your lowest safe height. Use them for backups, extra towels and things you don&#8217;t need every load, stored in baskets so they&#8217;re easy to pull down.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>2. Choose shallow shelves.</strong> A shallow shelf is easier to reach over a top-loader and less likely to get in the way of the lid. Deep shelves above a washer tend to collect things you can&#8217;t reach.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>3. Use the space above the dryer differently.</strong> If your dryer opens from the front, the space above it doesn&#8217;t have the same lid problem. A shelf or small folding surface above the dryer only can give you a work area while the washer lid stays free.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>4. Keep nothing on the washer lid.</strong> Bottles and baskets on the lid have to be moved for every load, and small items can fall into the drum. Give each one a home somewhere else.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Beside and Between</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="679" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-03-rolling-cart.jpg" alt="Slim white rolling cart with detergent bottles, dryer balls, clothespins and towels in a small laundry room" class="wp-image-693" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-03-rolling-cart.jpg 679w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-03-rolling-cart-199x300.jpg 199w" sizes="(max-width: 679px) 100vw, 679px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>5. Slide a slim rolling cart into the gap.</strong> The narrow space beside a washer or between the machines and the wall is perfect for a slim rolling cart. It holds detergent, dryer balls and clothespins, then rolls out when you need them.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>6. Add a narrow shelf unit at the side.</strong> If you have a few more inches, a tall narrow shelving unit beside the machines holds far more than a cart and keeps supplies at arm&#8217;s height.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>7. Store detergent where kids can&#8217;t reach.</strong> If you use liquid laundry packets, the U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission recommends keeping them <a href="https://www.cpsc.gov/s3fs-public/390_SafetyAlert_LaundryPackets_112021.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sealed in their original packaging and locked up out of a child&#8217;s sight and reach</a>. A high shelf or a latched cabinet beside the machines works well.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Walls and Doors</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-04-wall-storage.jpg" alt="Fold-down wall-mounted drying rack and a white pegboard with hooks and a small basket in a laundry room" class="wp-image-691" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-04-wall-storage.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-04-wall-storage-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-04-wall-storage-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-04-wall-storage-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>8. Install a fold-down drying rack.</strong> A wall-mounted drying rack folds flat when you don&#8217;t need it and gives you hanging space for delicates when you do, without taking any floor space.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>9. Use a pegboard for small tools.</strong> A pegboard holds a lint roller, a stain brush, a small basket and a spray bottle, and it&#8217;s easy to rearrange as your needs change.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>10. Add hooks for hanging clothes.</strong> A row of hooks or a short wall rod lets you hang shirts straight out of the dryer, so fewer things end up in a wrinkled pile.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>11. Put the back of the door to work.</strong> An over-the-door organizer holds cleaning cloths, dryer sheets and stain sprays in a spot that&#8217;s usually wasted. The same trick works in small bathrooms, as in our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/over-the-toilet-storage-ideas/">over the toilet storage ideas</a>.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Laundry Closets and Floor Space</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-05-closet-laundry.jpg" alt="Laundry closet with bifold doors open showing a top-loading washer and dryer, a tension rod with shirts and a shelf with baskets" class="wp-image-692" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-05-closet-laundry.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-05-closet-laundry-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-05-closet-laundry-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-05-closet-laundry-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>12. Hang a tension rod in a laundry closet.</strong> In a laundry closet, a tension rod above the dryer side gives you instant hanging space without drilling. Keep it above the washer&#8217;s lid line on the washer side.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>13. Use stackable or tall narrow hampers.</strong> Sorting hampers that stack or stand tall take less floor space than wide baskets. Label them lights, darks and towels to cut sorting time.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>14. Add a fold-down wall table.</strong> If you don&#8217;t have a counter, a small fold-down table on the wall gives you a folding surface that disappears when you&#8217;re done.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>15. Keep the dryer area clear.</strong> Don&#8217;t store baskets or supplies against the back of the dryer. The U.S. Fire Administration recommends <a href="https://www.usfa.fema.gov/downloads/pdf/publications/clothes_dryer_fire_safety_flyer.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cleaning the lint filter before and after each cycle</a> and checking that the vent behind the dryer isn&#8217;t crushed or restricted.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-06-before-after.jpg" alt="Small laundry room before and after: a cluttered room with clothes on the floor next to the same room organized with shelves, baskets and a rolling cart" class="wp-image-690" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-06-before-after.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-06-before-after-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-06-before-after-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laundry-06-before-after-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Mistakes in Small Laundry Rooms With a Top-Loader</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Hanging shelves before measuring the open lid.</strong> Measure first, drill second.</li>



<li><strong>Using the washer lid as a shelf.</strong> Everything on it has to move for every load.</li>



<li><strong>Buying a countertop meant for front-loaders.</strong> A counter over a top-loader blocks the lid unless it&#8217;s hinged or removable.</li>



<li><strong>Blocking the dryer vent.</strong> Keep the space behind and around the dryer free of storage.</li>



<li><strong>Keeping too much.</strong> A small laundry room works best with one of each supply in use and a small backup stash, not a whole shelf of half-used bottles. The same <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/closet-clean-out/">clean-out approach</a> we use for closets works here too.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Small Laundry Room With a Top-Loader FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Can you put a shelf above a top loading washer?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yes, as long as the bottom of the shelf sits above the height of the fully open lid, plus a few inches of hand room. Measure with the lid open before you install anything.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How high should a shelf be above a top loader?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Add the height of the closed washer to how far the lid rises when open, then add a few inches so you can reach in comfortably. That total is the lowest safe height for the bottom of the shelf. Check your washer&#8217;s spec sheet for its height with the lid open.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Can you put a countertop over a top-loading washer?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Not a fixed one, because it would block the lid. You can add a folding surface above the dryer only, use a fold-down wall table, or choose a hinged or removable top made for this purpose.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do I make a small laundry room more functional?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Keep the lid clear, move daily supplies to a slim cart beside the machines, put backups on high shelves, and use the walls for drying and hanging. Getting things off the floor makes the biggest difference.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Planning a full makeover or working with front-loaders instead? Our main guide to <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-laundry-room-ideas/">small laundry room ideas</a> covers 20 layout, storage and design ideas for every type of washer and dryer.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Planning around a top-loader? Our free <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/washer-dryer-space-calculator/">washer and dryer space calculator</a> checks the width, depth and lid clearance your space needs.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Planning a laundry closet instead of a full room? See our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/laundry-closet-ideas/">laundry closet ideas</a> for door options, closet sizes and storage that works around stacked or side-by-side machines.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A top-loading washer doesn&#8217;t mean giving up on storage. It just means working with the lid instead of against it: measure the open-lid height, keep that space clear, and use the gaps, walls and high shelves for everything else.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For the rest of the room, from sorting to folding, see our step-by-step guide on <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-organize-a-laundry-room/">how to organize a laundry room</a>. Save this guide to Pinterest so you have the clearance guide handy before you hang your first shelf.</p>



